</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: OVERVIEW -->
<div class="container">
<section id="overview">
  <h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">A portrait of 93 years of the Academy's most prestigious music honor.</p>

  <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented annually since 1934, making it one of the longest-running categories in Oscar history. Over 93 ceremonies, the award has recognized the composers whose music defined the emotional landscape of cinema&mdash;from Max Steiner's lush Golden Age orchestrations to Ludwig G&ouml;ransson's genre-crossing contemporary scores.</p>

  <p>This dataset comprises <strong>93 winning entries</strong> spanning from <strong>1934</strong> (Louis Silvers for <em>One Night of Love</em>) through <strong>2026</strong> (Ludwig G&ouml;ransson for <em>Sinners</em>). The 69 unique composer entries include both solo winners and collaborative teams. Notably, the category has undergone name changes and occasional splits (e.g., separate dramatic and comedy/musical score awards in certain years), but this analysis consolidates to one winner per ceremony year.</p>

  <p>Of the 93 total wins, <strong>24 (25.8%)</strong> went to composers who had already won at least once before&mdash;meaning roughly three out of every four ceremonies crowned a first-time winner. This balance between fresh voices and proven maestros forms one of the central tensions in the award's history.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: TOP COMPOSERS -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <h2>2. Most Awarded Composers</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">The titans of Oscar-winning film music, ranked by total wins.</p>

  <h3>The 4-Win Tier: A Three-Way Tie at the Top</h3>
  <div class="podium-grid">
    <div class="podium-card rank-1">
      <div class="rank-badge">T1</div>
      <div class="composer-name">John Barry</div>
      <div class="win-count">4 wins</div>
      <div class="win-years">1966 &middot; 1968 &middot; 1985 &middot; 1990</div>
    </div>
    <div class="podium-card rank-1">
      <div class="rank-badge">T1</div>
      <div class="composer-name">John Williams</div>
      <div class="win-count">4 wins</div>
      <div class="win-years">1975 &middot; 1977 &middot; 1982 &middot; 1993</div>
    </div>
    <div class="podium-card rank-1">
      <div class="rank-badge">T1</div>
      <div class="composer-name">Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</div>
      <div class="win-count">4 wins</div>
      <div class="win-years">2018 &middot; 2023 &middot; 2024 &middot; 2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>All Multi-Win Composers</h3>
  <table class="rankings-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Composer</th><th>Wins</th><th>Years</th><th>Span</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>T1</td><td>John Barry</td><td>4 <span class="win-bar" style="width:100px"></span></td><td>1966, 1968, 1985, 1990</td><td>24 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>T1</td><td>John Williams</td><td>4 <span class="win-bar" style="width:100px"></span></td><td>1975, 1977, 1982, 1993</td><td>18 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>T1</td><td>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</td><td>4 <span class="win-bar" style="width:100px"></span></td><td>2018, 2023, 2024, 2026</td><td>8 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Max Steiner</td><td>3 <span class="win-bar" style="width:75px"></span></td><td>1935, 1943, 1945</td><td>10 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Dimitri Tiomkin</td><td>3 <span class="win-bar" style="width:75px"></span></td><td>1952, 1954, 1958</td><td>6 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Maurice Jarre</td><td>3 <span class="win-bar" style="width:75px"></span></td><td>1962, 1965, 1984</td><td>22 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Alan Menken</td><td>3 <span class="win-bar" style="width:75px"></span></td><td>1989, 1991, 1992</td><td>3 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Alfred Newman</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>1944, 1955</td><td>11 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Miklos Rozsa</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>1946, 1959</td><td>13 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Franz Waxman</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>1950, 1951</td><td>1 year</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Hans Zimmer</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>1994, 2021</td><td>27 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Howard Shore</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>2001, 2003</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Gustavo Santaolalla</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>2005, 2006</td><td>1 year</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Alexandre Desplat</td><td>2 <span class="win-bar" style="width:50px"></span></td><td>2014, 2017</td><td>3 years</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Three composers sit at the pinnacle with 4 wins each&mdash;a statistical rarity that underscores how hard it is to win this award repeatedly. <strong>John Barry's</strong> wins span a remarkable 24 years (1966&ndash;1990), the widest career arc among the leaders. <strong>John Williams's</strong> four wins came over 18 years during his most prolific period, though he holds far more nominations than any other composer. <strong>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson</strong> achieved his four wins in just 8 years, the most concentrated dominance at the top tier in the award's history.</p>

  <p>Among the 3-win composers, <strong>Alan Menken's</strong> near-consecutive hat trick (1989, 1991, 1992) during Disney's renaissance stands out as the tightest cluster of wins. Meanwhile, <strong>Hans Zimmer's</strong> 27-year gap between wins (1994&ndash;2021) is the longest of any repeat winner in this dataset.</p>

