<!-- SECTION 3: TEMPORAL PATTERNS -->
<section id="temporal-patterns">
  <h2>3. Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">How the distribution of wins has shifted across nine decades of cinema.</p>

  <div class="chart-container wide">
    <canvas id="decadeChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Decade Breakdown</h3>

  <p><strong>1930s (6 ceremonies, 6 unique winners):</strong> Perfect diversity. Every winner was unique, reflecting a nascent award category with no established hierarchy. These early years drew from a small but varied pool of Hollywood's first studio-era composers.</p>

  <p><strong>1940s (10 ceremonies, 9 unique winners):</strong> Max Steiner emerged as the first repeat winner in this category, taking the award in 1935, 1943, and 1945 (two of those in the 1940s). Otherwise, the decade maintained high diversity with towering figures like Alfred Newman, Miklos Rozsa, Aaron Copland, and Franz Waxman each making their marks.</p>

  <p><strong>1950s (10 ceremonies, 7 unique winners):</strong> The most concentrated decade in the dataset's first half. Dimitri Tiomkin won three times (1952, 1954, 1958), and Franz Waxman scored back-to-back wins (1950, 1951)&mdash;the first consecutive wins by a single composer. This was the era when repeat winning became a visible pattern.</p>

  <p><strong>1960s (10 ceremonies, 8 unique winners):</strong> Maurice Jarre (1962, 1965) and John Barry (1966, 1968) each won twice, establishing the decade's dual-pole structure. The broader roster included Henry Mancini, Elmer Bernstein, and Burt Bacharach&mdash;a decade that blended classical orchestral tradition with pop-inflected modernity.</p>

  <p><strong>1970s (10 ceremonies, 9 unique winners):</strong> John Williams's twin <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977) wins launched a dominance that would extend into the next two decades. The rest of the 1970s featured remarkable one-off winners: Nino Rota, Marvin Hamlisch, Giorgio Moroder, and Georges Delerue.</p>

  <p><strong>1980s (10 ceremonies, 10 unique winners):</strong> The single most diverse decade&mdash;every ceremony produced a different winner. From Vangelis's electronic <em>Chariots of Fire</em> score (1981) to Herbie Hancock's jazz-fusion <em>Round Midnight</em> (1986) to Ryuichi Sakamoto's cross-cultural <em>The Last Emperor</em> (1987), the 1980s rewarded stylistic breadth like no other period.</p>

  <p><strong>1990s (10 ceremonies, 9 unique winners):</strong> Alan Menken's Disney dominance (1989&ndash;1992, with two wins in this decade) bookended by Williams's <em>Schindler's List</em> (1993) and Zimmer's <em>The Lion King</em> (1994). The late 1990s leaned international: Gabriel Yared (Lebanon/France), Nicola Piovani (Italy), Luis Bacalov (Argentina/Italy).</p>

  <p><strong>2000s (10 ceremonies, 8 unique winners):</strong> Howard Shore's <em>Lord of the Rings</em> wins (2001, 2003) and Gustavo Santaolalla's consecutive <em>Brokeback Mountain</em>/<em>Babel</em> victories (2005, 2006) provided the decade's repeat structure. The 2000s also brought the most globally diverse winner pool: Tan Dun (China), A.R. Rahman (India), Jan A.P. Kaczmarek (Poland).</p>

  <p><strong>2010s (10 ceremonies, 9 unique winners):</strong> Alexandre Desplat won twice (2014, 2017). The decade saw a striking variety of styles recognized: Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross's electronic <em>Social Network</em> score (2010), Ennio Morricone's late-career triumph (2015), Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir's cello-driven <em>Joker</em> (2019)&mdash;the first solo female winner in the category.</p>

  <p><strong>2020s (7 ceremonies through 2026, 5 unique winners):</strong> Already the most concentrated decade proportionally. Ludwig G&ouml;ransson has won 3 of 7 ceremonies (43%), an unprecedented dominance rate. Hans Zimmer's <em>Dune</em> (2021) added a second win spanning 27 years. With three years remaining, this decade's diversity metrics could still shift, but G&ouml;ransson's current trajectory is historically remarkable.</p>

