<!-- SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS -->
<section id="era-analysis">
  <h2>4. Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Four eras, four distinct characters in the story of Oscar-winning film music.</p>

  <div class="era-grid">
    <div class="era-card">
      <h4>The Golden Age</h4>
      <div class="era-years">1934&ndash;1960 &middot; 27 ceremonies</div>
      <p>The era of the great studio orchestrators. Lush, symphonic scores rooted in late-Romantic European tradition dominated. Composers were often studio employees writing at industrial scale. The field was small, and repeat winners emerged quickly: Max Steiner (3 wins), Dimitri Tiomkin (3), Franz Waxman (2), Alfred Newman (2), Miklos Rozsa (2).</p>
      <p class="era-composers"><strong>Defining winners:</strong> Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Erich Wolfgang Korngold, Alfred Newman, Aaron Copland, Miklos Rozsa</p>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card">
      <h4>The Silver Age</h4>
      <div class="era-years">1961&ndash;1980 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
      <p>The studio system's collapse brought independence and stylistic experimentation. Jazz (Henry Mancini, Burt Bacharach), pop (Giorgio Moroder), and international voices (Nino Rota, Francis Lai, Michel Legrand) infiltrated the category. John Williams's <em>Jaws</em> and <em>Star Wars</em> wins reaffirmed orchestral grandeur while pointing toward the blockbuster era.</p>
      <p class="era-composers"><strong>Defining winners:</strong> Maurice Jarre, John Barry, John Williams, Henry Mancini, Nino Rota, Burt Bacharach</p>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card">
      <h4>The Modern Era</h4>
      <div class="era-years">1981&ndash;2000 &middot; 20 ceremonies</div>
      <p>Maximum stylistic diversity. Electronic scores (Vangelis), jazz (Herbie Hancock), animated film music (Alan Menken's Disney trilogy), and blockbuster orchestral (Williams's <em>E.T.</em> and <em>Schindler's List</em>) all found favor. The 1980s produced 10 unique winners in 10 years&mdash;an unmatched record. International composers continued to break through (Sakamoto, Morricone-influenced Bacalov, Piovani).</p>
      <p class="era-composers"><strong>Defining winners:</strong> Vangelis, John Williams, Alan Menken, Hans Zimmer, Herbie Hancock, Ryuichi Sakamoto</p>
    </div>
    <div class="era-card">
      <h4>The Contemporary Era</h4>
      <div class="era-years">2001&ndash;2026 &middot; 26 ceremonies</div>
      <p>Globalization, genre fusion, and technology define this era. Winners range from Peter Jackson&rsquo;s epic collaborator Howard Shore to electronic pioneers Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross to Ludwig G&ouml;ransson's cross-genre mastery. The first solo female winner arrived (Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir, 2019). G&ouml;ransson's 4 wins in 8 years represent the most rapid accumulation in Oscar history.</p>
      <p class="era-composers"><strong>Defining winners:</strong> Howard Shore, Gustavo Santaolalla, Alexandre Desplat, Ludwig G&ouml;ransson, Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir, A.R. Rahman</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container medium">
    <canvas id="eraChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Cross-Era Patterns</h3>
  <p>A clear arc emerges: the Golden Age concentrated wins among a few prolific studio composers; the Silver Age dispersed them across stylistically varied independents; the Modern Era reached peak diversity; and the Contemporary Era has begun to reconcentrate around a new kind of dominant figure&mdash;one who crosses genre boundaries rather than mastering a single idiom.</p>
  <p>The repeat winner rate tells this story numerically: the Golden Age saw 37% of its ceremonies go to repeat winners (10 of 27), the Silver Age dropped to 20% (4 of 20), the Modern Era held at 15% (3 of 20), and the Contemporary Era has climbed back to 27% (7 of 26)&mdash;driven substantially by G&ouml;ransson and Shore.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: MILESTONES -->
<section id="notable-milestones">
  <h2>5. Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Landmark moments in the history of Oscar-winning scores.</p>

  <ul class="milestone-list">
    <li><strong>First winner (1934):</strong> Louis Silvers for <em>One Night of Love</em>&mdash;the inaugural Best Original Score recipient at the 6th Academy Awards.</li>
    <li><strong>First repeat winner:</strong> Max Steiner, who won in 1935 (<em>The Informer</em>), 1943 (<em>Now, Voyager</em>), and 1945 (<em>Since You Went Away</em>), establishing the template for multi-win composers.</li>
    <li><strong>First back-to-back winner:</strong> Franz Waxman, with consecutive wins for <em>Sunset Boulevard</em> (1950) and <em>A Place in the Sun</em> (1951).</li>
    <li><strong>Longest gap between wins (single composer):</strong> Hans Zimmer&mdash;27 years between <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) and <em>Dune</em> (2021), the widest span in this dataset.</li>
    <li><strong>Tightest win cluster:</strong> Alan Menken won 3 of 4 ceremonies from 1989 to 1992 (missing only 1990), Disney's animation renaissance in musical form.</li>
    <li><strong>Most diverse decade:</strong> The 1980s&mdash;10 different winners in 10 ceremonies. No other decade matches this perfect diversity.</li>
    <li><strong>First solo female winner:</strong> Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir for <em>Joker</em> (2019), a landmark in a category historically dominated by male composers.</li>
    <li><strong>Fastest to 4 wins:</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson reached 4 wins in just 8 years (2018&ndash;2026), compared to John Williams's 18 years and John Barry's 24 years.</li>
    <li><strong>Most recent winner (2026):</strong> Ludwig G&ouml;ransson for <em>Sinners</em>&mdash;his fourth win, tying Barry and Williams for the all-time record.</li>
    <li><strong>Consecutive same-composer wins:</strong> Gustavo Santaolalla (2005&ndash;2006 for <em>Brokeback Mountain</em> and <em>Babel</em>), Ludwig G&ouml;ransson (2023&ndash;2024), and Franz Waxman (1950&ndash;1951).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">What nine decades of data reveal about the trajectory of Oscar-winning film music.</p>

  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <h4>1. Dominance Is Cyclical, Not Linear</h4>
    <p>The Oscar scoring landscape oscillates between concentration and dispersal. The Golden Age's studio-era giants gave way to the Silver Age's eclecticism, which peaked in the 1980s' perfect diversity, only to begin reconcentrating in the 2020s around G&ouml;ransson. History suggests that the current concentration phase is temporary&mdash;new voices will emerge.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <h4>2. Four Wins Remains the Ceiling&mdash;For Now</h4>
    <p>No composer in 93 years has won more than four times. With G&ouml;ransson active and only 38 at the time of his 2026 win, the all-time record is within realistic reach. If he wins once more, it will break a statistical ceiling that has held since the category's inception.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <h4>3. Stylistic Range Has Replaced Stylistic Mastery</h4>
    <p>The Golden Age rewarded mastery of orchestral idiom; the Contemporary Era rewards versatility. G&ouml;ransson's wins span a Marvel superhero film, a historical drama (twice), and a period thriller. The Academy's taste has evolved toward composers who can move fluidly across genres.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <h4>4. The Category Is Becoming More Global</h4>
    <p>From an almost exclusively American/European winner pool through the 1980s, the 2000s onward brought Tan Dun (China), A.R. Rahman (India), Gustavo Santaolalla (Argentina), and G&ouml;ransson (Sweden). The globalization of film production is reflected in the diversification of composer nationalities.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="takeaway-box">
    <h4>5. First-Time Winners Still Dominate&mdash;But the Margin Is Narrowing</h4>
    <p>At 74.2%, the first-time winner rate remains high, but the 2020s are challenging this norm. With G&ouml;ransson winning 3 of the first 7 ceremonies this decade, the repeat-winner rate for the 2020s (42.9%) far exceeds any complete decade in the dataset. Whether this represents an anomaly or a structural shift will become clearer by the decade's end.</p>
  </div>
</section>

